---
import AppDocLayout from '../../layouts/AppDocLayout.astro';
---

<AppDocLayout
  title="Hydrate - Privacy Policy"
  description="Privacy policy for Hydrate - Simple Water Tracker iOS app."
  ogUrl="https://idlefusion.com/hydrate/privacy"
>
  <main>
    <section class="section-padding">
      <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">Privacy Policy</h1>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--text-secondary)] mb-12">Hydrate - Simple Water Tracker &middot; Effective October 22, 2026</p>

        <div class="space-y-8 text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed">
          <div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-3">The Short Version</h2>
            <p>Hydrate does not collect, store, or transmit any personal data. Your water intake stays on your device.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-3">Data Storage</h2>
            <p>Your drinks, goals, streaks and settings are stored on your device, where the Hydrate app and its widgets share them. No data is sent to any server, cloud service, or third party. There are no user accounts and no sign-up required.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-3">Apple Watch</h2>
            <p>If you use Hydrate on Apple Watch, your iPhone sends today's goal and drinks to your paired watch, and drinks you log on the watch are sent to your iPhone. This happens directly between your own devices through Apple's WatchConnectivity; nothing goes to us or anyone else.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-3">Apple Health</h2>
            <p class="mb-3">If you connect Apple Health in Settings, Hydrate saves each drink you log to Apple Health as Water (dietary water), and deletes or updates it there when you delete or change the drink in Hydrate. Hydrate only writes Water; it doesn't read any data from Apple Health. Health data is governed by <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--accent-primary)] hover:underline">Apple's privacy policy</a> and never leaves your device through Hydrate.</p>
            <p>If you don't connect Apple Health, or turn it off, the app works the same and your drinks stay in Hydrate only. You can pause saving in Hydrate's Settings, or change access any time in the Health app.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-3">Analytics & Tracking</h2>
            <p>Hydrate contains no analytics frameworks, no ad SDKs, no tracking pixels, and no telemetry of any kind. We do not use any third-party services that collect data.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-3">Network Usage</h2>
            <p>Hydrate makes no network requests. The app works entirely offline. The only network activity associated with the app is through the App Store for updates.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-3">Notifications</h2>
            <p>If you enable hydration reminders, notifications are scheduled locally on your device. No data is sent to any server to deliver notifications.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-3">Children's Privacy</h2>
            <p>Hydrate does not knowingly collect any information from anyone, including children under the age of 13.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-3">Data Deletion</h2>
            <p>When you delete the Hydrate app, its data is permanently deleted from your device. You can also delete every drink in Settings. Drinks already saved to Apple Health stay there until you delete them in the Health app. There is no cloud copy to remove because we never had your data in the first place.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-3">Changes to This Policy</h2>
            <p>If this policy changes, we will update this page with the new effective date. Since Hydrate collects no data, meaningful changes are unlikely.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</AppDocLayout>
